{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Give yourselves a sign.</b> Rashi: a sign to inform him that there is צרוך (need/power) in the one who sends you. צרוך in the sense of ability and dominion, as stated in the Aggadah: why were idolatry called 'elohim'? Rabbi Yosi says: so there be no opening for the mouth to say they were not called elohim. Had they been called elohim, there would be צרוך in them. 'They sacrifice to shedim, not eloh' (Deut. 32:17) — we translate: 'who are destined to err' — for there is no צרוך in them." "<b>And cast it before Pharaoh, let it become a tannin.</b> Before Pharaoh — for Pharaoh boasted in his own eyes and called himself a tannin, as it is written, 'the great tannin' (Ezek. 29:3). HaShem said to Moses: go, say — just as this staff becomes a tannin and swallows staffs, and in the end returns to being dry wood, so you swallow the twelve staffs of the tribes of Israel, and in the end you shall be as dry wood and die." "<b>Let it become a tannin.</b> This passage was already stated in the portion of Shemot: 'See all the signs that I have placed in your hand, and perform them before Pharaoh,' and is repeated here on account of something new in it. And what is the novelty? Not to perform before Pharaoh anything except 'let it become a tannin.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:56:29.376513705Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}